  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PPIUF supported Samoa&#x2019;s initiatives to combat the increasing prevalence of non- communicable diseases (NCDs) through strengthening its health system, to improve the health and wellbeing of its people, including in rural communities. This included educational initiatives for schools to promote healthy lifestyles and reduce risks of developing NCDs at a young age and increased access to NCD screening through community level screening programs across 4 additional districts allowing for timely diagnosis, referral and treatment of high-risk individuals. The program continued to support the procurement of essential technologies and engagement of multi-disciplinary teams consisting of a physician, nurse, public health officer, midwife, nutritionist and data assistant for rural district hospitals to provide better primary health care. Additionally, support was rendered to increase Samoa&#x2019;s capacity to manage healthcare waste and an increased capability of its e-Health system to improve accessibility to up-to date patient medical records across all of its public health facilities allowing physicians to administer timely and appropriate treatments.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PPIUF supported community led infrastructure, human development and waste management initiatives in the Solomon Islands through AUD11.7m to the World Bank for the Community Access and Urban Services Enhancement Project (CAUSE). Since commencing in 2018, CAUSE has trained and engaged 8,500 unemployed with equal representation of females (51%) and youth (51%) who worked on the delivery of 213 climate-resilient infrastructure projects (including Jacobs ladders, sidewalks, road maintenance and improvements to Honiara&#x2019;s Central Market) improving access to quality infrastructure, distributed over SBD26m in wages supporting the community and vulnerable people, and collected over 66,500m3 of urban waste. In 2024, CAUSE completed the remaining 13 community access infrastructure subprojects and finalised road repair and ongoing maintenance activities in Honiara, covering 13.5 kilometres. CAUSE delivered new market facilities in West Honiara with the opening of the White River Market in July 2024 providing increased safety for communities, creating additional jobs and fostering economic growth. Building on the success and architecture of CAUSE&#x2019;s first phase, Australia committed AUD30m to the World Bank for the second phase (CAUSE 2, 2024-2030) that will continue to significantly support communities in Solomon Islands.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
